<th id="A2.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A2.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation</span></th>
<th id="A2.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A2.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Description</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The target model.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The set of data used to train the model <math id="A2.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">A data point and the <math id="A2.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th data point, respectively.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M,M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M,M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">true membership label for a data point <math id="A2.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (1 if it is in traning set <math id="A2.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>, otherwise 0).</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}},\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>cal</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}},\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The calibration set and the test set, respectively.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,m</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The number of samples in the calibration and test sets, respectively.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(X;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(X;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The detection score for data point <math id="A2.T2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., perplexity). Lower is more member-like.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The null hypothesis that test point <math id="A2.T2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n+j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n+j}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-member (<math id="A2.T2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{n+j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{n+j}=0</annotation></semantics></math>).</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The selected subset of indices from <math id="A2.T2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> rejected as null hypotheses.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.11" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{FDR}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>FDR</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{FDR}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">False Discovery Rate: The expected proportion of false discoveries in <math id="A2.T2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.12" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Power}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Power</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Power}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">Power: The expected proportion of true members correctly identified in <math id="A2.T2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.13" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.13.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.13.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The target False Discovery Rate (FDR) level.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.14" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.14.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.14.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The true proportion of training members in the test set.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.15" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.15.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.15.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">An estimate of the data usage proportion <math id="A2.T2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.16" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.16.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.16.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The initial conformal p-value for the <math id="A2.T2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th test point.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.17" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.17.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.17.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The scaled p-value, calculated as <math id="A2.T2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{test}})p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{test}})p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.18" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.18.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.18.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">A generic estimator function for the data usage proportion.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.19" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.19.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.19.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The subtraction-based estimator for <math id="A2.T2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.20" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.20.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{\text{mom}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>mom</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{\text{mom}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.20.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The adjusted moment-based estimator for <math id="A2.T2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.21" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.21.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.21.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">A region of scores used by the subtraction estimator.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.22" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.22.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.22.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">A quantile hyperparameter used to define the region <math id="A2.T2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.23" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.23.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0},\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0},\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.23.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The true mean of detection scores for non-members and members.</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.24" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T2.2.24.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{0},\hat{\mu}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{0},\hat{\mu}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T2.2.24.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">The sample mean of detection scores for non-members and members.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Proofs</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>The proof of <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 3.2 Estimate data usage proportion ‣ 3 methodology ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="Thmproposition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmproposition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">sub</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subtraction estimator defined above. Assuming the test data points are i.i.d. draws from the test distribution, the expectation of the ratio of the true non-member proportion to the estimated non-member proportion is bounded by 1. Formally,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]\leq 1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">sub</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">The expectation is taken over the randomness of both the calibration set <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>cal</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}</annotation></semantics></math> (which determines the random region <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the test set. We use the law of total expectation by first conditioning on a fixed region <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note that <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen such that <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathds{1}\{T(X_{i})\in\mathcal{R}\}=\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathds{1}\{T(X_{i})\in\mathcal{R}\}=\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K=\sum_{j=1}^{m}\mathds{1}\{T(X_{n+j})\in\mathcal{R}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\sum_{j=1}^{m}\mathds{1}\{T(X_{n+j})\in\mathcal{R}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the random count of test points in the region <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}=\frac{K+1}{(m+1)\eta}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}=\frac{K+1}{(m+1)\eta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">The expression inside the expectation is therefore:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}=\frac{\eta(m+1)(1-\pi_{\text{test}})}{K+1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}=\frac{\eta(m+1)(1-\pi_{\text{test}})}{K+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Now, we take the conditional expectation with respect to the test set, for a fixed region <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{K|\mathcal{R}}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]=\eta(m+1)(1-\pi_{\text{test}})\cdot\mathbb{E}_{K|\mathcal{R}}\left[\frac{1}{K+1}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{K|\mathcal{R}}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]=\eta(m+1)(1-\pi_{\text{test}})\cdot\mathbb{E}_{K|\mathcal{R}}\left[\frac{1}{K+1}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">The count <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> follows a binomial distribution <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\sim\text{Bin}(m,p_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Bin</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\sim\text{Bin}(m,p_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{K}=\int_{\mathcal{R}}p(t_{\text{test}})dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{K}=\int_{\mathcal{R}}p(t_{\text{test}})dt</annotation></semantics></math>. A key property of the binomial distribution is the exact closed-form solution for the expectation of <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(K+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(K+1)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{K|\mathcal{R}}\left[\frac{1}{K+1}\right]=\frac{1-(1-p_{K})^{m+1}}{(m+1)p_{K}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{K|\mathcal{R}}\left[\frac{1}{K+1}\right]=\frac{1-(1-p_{K})^{m+1}}{(m+1)p_{K}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">Substituting this back into our expression for the conditional expectation gives:</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{K|\mathcal{R}}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{K|\mathcal{R}}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\eta(m+1)(1-\pi_{\text{test}})\left(\frac{1-(1-p_{K})^{m+1}}{(m+1)p_{K}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\eta(m+1)(1-\pi_{\text{test}})\left(\frac{1-(1-p_{K})^{m+1}}{(m+1)p_{K}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\eta(1-\pi_{\text{test}})}{p_{K}}\left(1-(1-p_{K})^{m+1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\eta(1-\pi_{\text{test}})}{p_{K}}\left(1-(1-p_{K})^{m+1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.6.1" class="ltx_text">From the initial problem setup, we have the inequality <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{K}\geqslant(1-\pi_{\text{test}})\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{K}\geqslant(1-\pi_{\text{test}})\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\eta(1-\pi_{\text{test}})}{p_{K}}\leqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>⩽</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\eta(1-\pi_{\text{test}})}{p_{K}}\leqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, since <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{K}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{K}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geqslant 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geqslant 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the term <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-(1-p_{K})^{m+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-(1-p_{K})^{m+1})</annotation></semantics></math> is also less than or equal to 1. The product of two non-negative numbers that are both less than or equal to 1 must also be less than or equal to 1. Thus, for any fixed region <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, the conditional expectation is bounded:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{K|\mathcal{R}}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]\leqslant 1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{K|\mathcal{R}}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]\leqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.7.1" class="ltx_text">Since this inequality holds for any region <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> that could be realized from the calibration set, the total expectation over both the calibration and test sets must also be bounded by 1:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{R}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{K|\mathcal{R}}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]\right]\leqslant\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{R}}[1]=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{R}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{K|\mathcal{R}}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]\right]\leqslant\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{R}}[1]=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.8.1" class="ltx_text">This completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>The proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.2 Estimate data usage proportion ‣ 3 methodology ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Classical FDR Control under PRDS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benjamini &amp; Yekutieli, 2001</a>)</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a set of p-values <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{j}\}_{j=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{j}\}_{j=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> hypothesis tests, of which <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are true null hypotheses. If the p-values corresponding to the true nulls are valid (i.e., they are super-uniformly distributed under the null), and if the entire p-value vector satisfies the Positive Regression Dependency on a Subset (PRDS) property, then the standard Benjamini-Hochberg (BH) procedure at a target level <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> controls the False Discovery Rate (FDR) such that:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{FDR}\leq\frac{m_{0}}{m}\alpha." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>FDR</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{FDR}\leq\frac{m_{0}}{m}\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4</span></span><span id="Thmproposition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(FDR Control for the BH Procedure on <math id="Thmproposition4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmproposition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the p-values <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{j}\}_{j=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{j}\}_{j=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be constructed as defined in <a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.1 Provable Training Data Identification ‣ 3 methodology ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. Assume that each sample’s membership status in the test set <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an independent Bernoulli trial, where the probability of being a member (<math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1</annotation></semantics></math>) is <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Further assume that the p-value vector <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{1},\dots,p_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{1},\dots,p_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the PRDS property.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the standard Benjamini-Hochberg (BH) procedure is applied directly to these p-values <math id="Thmproposition4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{j}^{0}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{j}^{0}\}</annotation></semantics></math> at a target level <math id="Thmproposition4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, then its FDR is controlled such that:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{FDR}\leqslant\alpha\cdot(1-\pi_{\text{test}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>FDR</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{FDR}\leqslant\alpha\cdot(1-\pi_{\text{test}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, we verify that the p-values <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.1 Provable Training Data Identification ‣ 3 methodology ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> satisfy the preconditions of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2 (Classical FDR Control under PRDS ( , )). ‣ C.2 The proof of ‣ Appendix C Proofs ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any true null hypothesis <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{n+j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{n+j}=0</annotation></semantics></math>), the p-value <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed using a calibration set composed entirely of non-members. According to the principles of conformal prediction, when the test sample <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n+j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n+j}</annotation></semantics></math> is also a non-member, its nonconformity score <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{V}_{n+j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{V}_{n+j}</annotation></semantics></math> is exchangeable with the scores from the calibration set. This construction guarantees that <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is super-uniformly distributed under the null hypothesis, meaning <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(p_{j}\leqslant t\mid j\in\mathcal{H}_{0})\leqslant t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⩽</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(p_{j}\leqslant t\mid j\in\mathcal{H}_{0})\leqslant t</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The PRDS property is validated by Lemma B.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin &amp; Candès (2023)</a></cite>. Since both conditions are met, we can apply the conclusion of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2 (Classical FDR Control under PRDS ( , )). ‣ C.2 The proof of ‣ Appendix C Proofs ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note in our problem formulation, the number of non-members <math id="A3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random variable. Based on our assumption, <math id="A3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> follows a Binomial distribution, <math id="A3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}\sim B(m,1-\pi_{\text{test}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}\sim B(m,1-\pi_{\text{test}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using the law of total expectation, we can first condition on <math id="A3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and then take the expectation over its distribution:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{FDR}=\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{FDP}]=\mathbb{E}_{m_{0}}\left[\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{FDP}\mid m_{0}]\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>FDR</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>FDP</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>FDP</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{FDR}=\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{FDP}]=\mathbb{E}_{m_{0}}\left[\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{FDP}\mid m_{0}]\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">From Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2 (Classical FDR Control under PRDS ( , )). ‣ C.2 The proof of ‣ Appendix C Proofs ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we have the bound for the inner expectation: <math id="A3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{FDP}\mid m_{0}]\leq\frac{m_{0}}{m}\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>FDP</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{FDP}\mid m_{0}]\leq\frac{m_{0}}{m}\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting this into the equation gives:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{FDR}\leq\mathbb{E}_{m_{0}}\left[\frac{m_{0}}{m}\alpha\right]=\frac{\alpha}{m}\mathbb{E}[m_{0}]=\alpha\cdot(1-\pi_{\text{test}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>FDR</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{FDR}\leq\mathbb{E}_{m_{0}}\left[\frac{m_{0}}{m}\alpha\right]=\frac{\alpha}{m}\mathbb{E}[m_{0}]=\alpha\cdot(1-\pi_{\text{test}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">This completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">With these results, we are ready to prove the main theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof"><span id="A3.SS2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.2 Estimate data usage proportion ‣ 3 methodology ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span>.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be the rejection set from Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.1 Provable Training Data Identification ‣ 3 methodology ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The FDR is defined as <math id="A3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FDR}=\mathbb{E}[\text{FDP}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>FDR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>FDP</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FDR}=\mathbb{E}[\text{FDP}]</annotation></semantics></math>. We analyze this expectation by conditioning on the random estimator <math id="A3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the law of total expectation:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FDR}=\mathbb{E}_{\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\left[\mathbb{E}\left[\text{FDP}\big|\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}\right]\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>FDR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mtext>FDP</mtext><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FDR}=\mathbb{E}_{\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\left[\mathbb{E}\left[\text{FDP}\big|\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">For a fixed value of the estimator <math id="A3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the procedure’s rejection rule on the adjusted p-values <math id="A3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tilde{p}_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tilde{p}_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to applying the standard BH procedure to the original p-values <math id="A3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> at a modified, random level of <math id="A3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}=\alpha/(1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}=\alpha/(1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We can now apply <a href="#Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4 (FDR Control for the BH Procedure on 𝑝_𝑗). ‣ C.2 The proof of ‣ Appendix C Proofs ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> to analyze the inner conditional expectation. The proportion’s unconditional form states that a standard BH procedure at level <math id="A3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> provides an FDR guarantee of <math id="A3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the conditional FDR of our procedure is bounded as:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\text{FDP}\big|\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}\right]\leqslant(1-\pi_{\text{sub}})\cdot\alpha^{\prime}=\alpha\cdot\frac{1-\pi_{\text{sub}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mtext>FDP</mtext><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\text{FDP}\big|\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}\right]\leqslant(1-\pi_{\text{sub}})\cdot\alpha^{\prime}=\alpha\cdot\frac{1-\pi_{\text{sub}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we take the outer expectation with respect to the randomness in <math id="A3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and apply the <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 3.2 Estimate data usage proportion ‣ 3 methodology ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>:</span></p>
<table id="A3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FDR}\leqslant\mathbb{E}_{\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\left[\alpha\cdot\frac{1-\pi_{\text{sub}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]=\alpha\cdot\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]\leqslant\alpha" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>FDR</mtext><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FDR}\leqslant\mathbb{E}_{\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\left[\alpha\cdot\frac{1-\pi_{\text{sub}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]=\alpha\cdot\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]\leqslant\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">This completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>The Proof of <a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2. ‣ Improving power by adjusted moment estimator. ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove the consistency of <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{\text{mom}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>mom</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{\text{mom}}</annotation></semantics></math> by relying on the Weak Law of Large Numbers (WLLN) and the Continuous Mapping Theorem (CMT). Let <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0},\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0},\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{0}^{2},\sigma_{1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{0}^{2},\sigma_{1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the true means and variances of the detection scores for the non-member and member populations, respectively. The true mean of the test set is <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{test}}=(1-\pi_{\text{test}})\mu_{0}+\pi_{\text{test}}\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{test}}=(1-\pi_{\text{test}})\mu_{0}+\pi_{\text{test}}\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we define the true non-member proportion as <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}=1-\pi_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}=1-\pi_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Under the assumption of finite moments, the WLLN ensures that the sample means <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{0},\hat{\mu}_{1},\hat{\mu}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{0},\hat{\mu}_{1},\hat{\mu}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> and sample variances <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{0}^{2},\hat{\sigma}_{1}^{2},\hat{\sigma}_{\text{test}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>test</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{0}^{2},\hat{\sigma}_{1}^{2},\hat{\sigma}_{\text{test}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> converge in probability to their true population counterparts. Since the raw estimator <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous function of these sample means, the CMT implies its convergence in probability. Specifically, assuming <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}\neq\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}\neq\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for the score to be informative, we have</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}=\frac{\hat{\mu}_{1}-\hat{\mu}_{\text{test}}}{\hat{\mu}_{1}-\hat{\mu}_{0}}\xrightarrow{p}\frac{\mu_{1}-\mu_{\text{test}}}{\mu_{1}-\mu_{0}}=\frac{\mu_{1}-((1-\pi_{\text{test}})\mu_{0}+\pi_{\text{test}}\mu_{1})}{\mu_{1}-\mu_{0}}=\pi_{0}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑝</mo></mover><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}=\frac{\hat{\mu}_{1}-\hat{\mu}_{\text{test}}}{\hat{\mu}_{1}-\hat{\mu}_{0}}\xrightarrow{p}\frac{\mu_{1}-\mu_{\text{test}}}{\mu_{1}-\mu_{0}}=\frac{\mu_{1}-((1-\pi_{\text{test}})\mu_{0}+\pi_{\text{test}}\mu_{1})}{\mu_{1}-\mu_{0}}=\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Next, we consider the bias-correction term <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext>Var</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})=\frac{1}{(\hat{\mu}_{1}-\hat{\mu}_{0})^{2}}\left[\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{2}\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{0}^{2}}{n_{0}}+(1-\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})^{2}\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}}+\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{\text{test}}^{2}}{m}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext>Var</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>test</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})=\frac{1}{(\hat{\mu}_{1}-\hat{\mu}_{0})^{2}}\left[\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{2}\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{0}^{2}}{n_{0}}+(1-\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})^{2}\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}}+\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{\text{test}}^{2}}{m}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}=|\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}^{0}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>cal</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}=|\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}^{0}|</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}=|\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}^{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>cal</mtext><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}=|\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}^{1}|</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=|\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=|\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}|</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0},n_{1},m\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0},n_{1},m\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the estimated variance <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext>Var</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})</annotation></semantics></math> converges in probability to zero because its constituent terms are scaled by <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n_{0},1/n_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n_{0},1/n_{1},</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/m</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> converges in probability to the non-zero constant <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the entire correction term converges to zero by Slutsky’s theorem. It follows that <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}=1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\frac{\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{3}}\xrightarrow{p}1/\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext>Var</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑝</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}=1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\frac{\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{3}}\xrightarrow{p}1/\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, since the final estimator is a continuous transformation of <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, another application of the CMT yields the desired result:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{\text{mom}}=1-\frac{1}{\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}}\xrightarrow{p}1-\frac{1}{1/\pi_{0}}=1-\pi_{0}=\pi_{\text{test}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>mom</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mfrac></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑝</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{\text{mom}}=1-\frac{1}{\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}}\xrightarrow{p}1-\frac{1}{1/\pi_{0}}=1-\pi_{0}=\pi_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">This completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Detail Derivation for Adjusted Moment Estimator</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first define the raw moment estimator, <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows:</p>
<table id="A4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}=\frac{\hat{\mu}_{1}-\hat{\mu}_{\text{test}}}{\hat{\mu}_{1}-\hat{\mu}_{0}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}=\frac{\hat{\mu}_{1}-\hat{\mu}_{\text{test}}}{\hat{\mu}_{1}-\hat{\mu}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{0},\hat{\mu}_{1},\text{ and }\hat{\mu}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{0},\hat{\mu}_{1},\text{ and }\hat{\mu}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the sample means from their respective datasets. By the Law of Large Numbers and the Continuous Mapping Theorem, <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a consistent estimator of the true proportion <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="A4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}\xrightarrow{p}\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑝</mo></mover><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}\xrightarrow{p}\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, our interest lies in the expectation of its reciprocal, <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the function <math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=1/x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=1/x</annotation></semantics></math> is convex, by Jensen’s inequality, we have <math id="A4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}]&gt;1/E[\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}]&gt;1/E[\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which indicates that <math id="A4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a biased estimator of <math id="A4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is likely to obtain an underieable propert such that <math id="A4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}]&gt;1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}]&gt;1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math> (see the <a href="#A3.E16" title="In Proof of . ‣ C.2 The proof of ‣ Appendix C Proofs ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>, which requires the oppsite property).</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify this bias, we perform a second-order Taylor expansion of the function <math id="A4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})=1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})=1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math> around the true value <math id="A4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}}\approx\frac{1}{\pi_{0}}-\frac{1}{\pi_{0}^{2}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\pi_{0})+\frac{1}{\pi_{0}^{3}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\pi_{0})^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}}\approx\frac{1}{\pi_{0}}-\frac{1}{\pi_{0}^{2}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\pi_{0})+\frac{1}{\pi_{0}^{3}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\pi_{0})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Taking the expectation of both sides, we get:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\left[\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}}\right]\approx E\left[\frac{1}{\pi_{0}}\right]-\frac{1}{\pi_{0}^{2}}E[\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\pi_{0}]+\frac{1}{\pi_{0}^{3}}E[(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\pi_{0})^{2}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\left[\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}}\right]\approx E\left[\frac{1}{\pi_{0}}\right]-\frac{1}{\pi_{0}^{2}}E[\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\pi_{0}]+\frac{1}{\pi_{0}^{3}}E[(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\pi_{0})^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math> is asymptotically unbiased, <math id="A4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\pi_{0}]\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\pi_{0}]\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math>. By the definition of variance, <math id="A4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\pi_{0})^{2}]\approx\text{Var}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}-\pi_{0})^{2}]\approx\text{Var}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the equation simplifies to:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\left[\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}}\right]\approx\frac{1}{\pi_{0}}+\frac{\text{Var}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})}{\pi_{0}^{3}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\left[\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}}\right]\approx\frac{1}{\pi_{0}}+\frac{\text{Var}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})}{\pi_{0}^{3}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p3.4" class="ltx_p">The term <math id="A4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Var}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})/\pi_{0}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Var}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})/\pi_{0}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is the leading source of bias. It is a positive value, indicating that the naive estimator usually overestimates <math id="A4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="A4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\mu_{0},\mu_{1},\mu_{\text{test}})=\frac{\mu_{1}-\mu_{\text{test}}}{\mu_{1}-\mu_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\mu_{0},\mu_{1},\mu_{\text{test}})=\frac{\mu_{1}-\mu_{\text{test}}}{\mu_{1}-\mu_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. According to the first-order Delta method, the variance of <math id="A4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated as:</p>
<table id="A4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Var}(\hat{\pi}_{raw})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Var}(\hat{\pi}_{raw})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx\left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial\mu_{0}}\right)^{2}\text{Var}(\hat{\mu}_{0})+\left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial\mu_{1}}\right)^{2}\text{Var}(\hat{\mu}_{1})+\left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial\mu_{\text{test}}}\right)^{2}\text{Var}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Var</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx\left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial\mu_{0}}\right)^{2}\text{Var}(\hat{\mu}_{0})+\left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial\mu_{1}}\right)^{2}\text{Var}(\hat{\mu}_{1})+\left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial\mu_{\text{test}}}\right)^{2}\text{Var}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{(\mu_{1}-\mu_{0})^{2}}\left[\pi_{0}^{2}\frac{\sigma_{0}^{2}}{n_{0}}+(1-\pi_{0})^{2}\frac{\sigma_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}}+\frac{\sigma_{\text{test}}^{2}}{m}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>test</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{(\mu_{1}-\mu_{0})^{2}}\left[\pi_{0}^{2}\frac{\sigma_{0}^{2}}{n_{0}}+(1-\pi_{0})^{2}\frac{\sigma_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}}+\frac{\sigma_{\text{test}}^{2}}{m}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p3.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the population variance and <math id="A4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sample size.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, to obtain the estimator for the variance, <math id="A4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext>Var</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})</annotation></semantics></math>, we replace all unknown population parameters with their corresponding sample estimates:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})=\frac{1}{(\hat{\mu}_{1}-\hat{\mu}_{0})^{2}}\left[\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{2}\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{0}^{2}}{n_{0}}+(1-\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})^{2}\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}}+\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{\text{test}}^{2}}{m}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext>Var</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>test</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})=\frac{1}{(\hat{\mu}_{1}-\hat{\mu}_{0})^{2}}\left[\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{2}\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{0}^{2}}{n_{0}}+(1-\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})^{2}\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}}+\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{\text{test}}^{2}}{m}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, we construct a corrected estimator, <math id="A4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is designed to subtract the estimated leading bias term:</p>
<table id="A4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}=\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}}-\frac{\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{3}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext>Var</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}=\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}}-\frac{\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{3}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">From the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.2 Estimate data usage proportion ‣ 3 methodology ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have the bound <math id="A4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FDR}\leqslant\alpha\cdot\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>FDR</mtext><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FDR}\leqslant\alpha\cdot\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1-\pi_{\text{test}}}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}=1-\pi_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}=1-\pi_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the true proportion of non-members and <math id="A4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{0}=1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{mom}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>mom</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{0}=1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{mom}}</annotation></semantics></math> be our final estimate. The inequality can be rewritten as <math id="A4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FDR}\leqslant\alpha\cdot\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\pi_{0}}{\hat{\pi}_{0}}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>FDR</mtext><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FDR}\leqslant\alpha\cdot\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\pi_{0}}{\hat{\pi}_{0}}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As established in <a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2. ‣ Improving power by adjusted moment estimator. ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, our estimator is consistent, meaning <math id="A4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{\text{mom}}\xrightarrow{p}\pi_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>mom</mtext></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑝</mo></mover><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{\text{mom}}\xrightarrow{p}\pi_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the sample sizes grow. This implies that <math id="A4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{0}\xrightarrow{p}\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑝</mo></mover><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{0}\xrightarrow{p}\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and by the continuous mapping theorem, the ratio <math id="A4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\pi_{0}}{\hat{\pi}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\pi_{0}}{\hat{\pi}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> converges in probability to 1. This ensures that the FDR is controlled asymptotically.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">More critically for practical applications, the bias correction helps ensure control in finite samples. As derived previously, the term <math id="A4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\hat{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\hat{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in our method is calculated as</p>
<table id="A4.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{mom}}}=\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}=\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}}-\frac{\widehat{\mathrm{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{3}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>mom</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Var</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{1-\hat{\pi}_{\text{mom}}}=\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}=\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}}-\frac{\widehat{\mathrm{Var}}(\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}})}{\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}^{3}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Since the naive estimator <math id="A4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>raw</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\hat{\pi}_{0,\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math> overestimates <math id="A4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on average due to Jensen’s inequality, we subtract a positive term to correct for this bias. This correction makes our final estimator <math id="A4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{1/\pi_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> conservative, such that its expectation is driven to be less than or equal to the true value <math id="A4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. This conservatism is precisely what leads to</p>
<table id="A4.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\pi_{0}}{\hat{\pi}_{0}}\right]=\pi_{0}\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}_{0}}\right]\lesssim\pi_{0}\cdot\frac{1}{\pi_{0}}=1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\pi_{0}}{\hat{\pi}_{0}}\right]=\pi_{0}\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}_{0}}\right]\lesssim\pi_{0}\cdot\frac{1}{\pi_{0}}=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p8.3" class="ltx_p">thereby achieving valid FDR control in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from <a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.2 Problem statement ‣ 2 Preliminary ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> that the false discovery rate (FDR) is defined as the expectation of the false discovery proportion,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{FDP}:=\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{m}\mathds{1}\{M_{n+j}=0,\,j\in\mathcal{S}\}}{\max(|\mathcal{S}|,1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>FDP</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{FDP}:=\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{m}\mathds{1}\{M_{n+j}=0,\,j\in\mathcal{S}\}}{\max(|\mathcal{S}|,1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we report the empirical FDR by averaging FDP over 1000 times of <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.1 Provable Training Data Identification ‣ 3 methodology ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For all experiments conducted on the WikiMIA benchmark, input text sequences were uniformly processed to a fixed length of 32 tokens. As for the experiment on VLMs, the generated token length is set to be 32.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Data Split Setup</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unless otherwise specified (e.g., when varying <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>), in each trial we randomly split the dataset into two equal halves. All non-members from one half are used to construct the calibration set <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>cal</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the other half serves as the test set <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the experiment in <a href="#S4.F4" title='In Robustness of FDR Control to Variations in 𝜋_"test". ‣ 4.2 Main results ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, in each trial, we further subsample the test set according to <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we select <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{test}}\cdot|\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{0}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{test}}\cdot|\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{0}|</annotation></semantics></math> points from <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\pi_{\text{test}})\cdot|\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{0}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\pi_{\text{test}})\cdot|\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{0}|</annotation></semantics></math> points from <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the non-member and member subsets of <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the adjusted moments estimator experiment in <a href="#S5.F6" title="In Improving power by adjusted moment estimator. ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we again split the dataset into two equal halves, assigning one to <math id="A5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>test</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the other to <math id="A5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>cal</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Additional Results</h2>

<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Evaluation of Subtraction Estimator</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated our subtraction estimator using GPT-NeoX-20B on the ArXivTection dataset.
The results in <a href="#A6.T3" title="In F.1 Evaluation of Subtraction Estimator ‣ Appendix F Additional Results ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> show a consistently negative bias (<math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}]-\pi_{\text{test}}\leqslant 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\hat{\pi}_{\text{sub}}]-\pi_{\text{test}}\leqslant 0</annotation></semantics></math>) for all detection scores and tested values of <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This confirms that our estimator is conservative, as shown in <a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Estimate data usage proportion ‣ 3 methodology ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>. Since the bias was always negative, we report its magnitude in the table.</p>
</div>
