<section id="A6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.4 </span>Sensitive analysis to the hyperparameter <math id="A6.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the impact on the hyperparameter <math id="A6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, we conduct experiments with GPT-NeoX-20B on ArxivTection varing <math id="A6.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> in { 0.01, 0.05, 0.1, 0.5 }. The results in <a href="#A6.F8" title="In F.4 Sensitive analysis to the hyperparameter 𝜂 ‣ Appendix F Additional Results ‣ High-Power Training Data Identification with Provable Statistical Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> show that our method robustly controls the FDR across all tested values of <math id="A6.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A6.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09717v1/gpt_neox_20b_arxivtection_ppl_quantile_comparison.svg" id="A6.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/368;" width="548" height="368"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Perplexity</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A6.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09717v1/gpt_neox_20b_arxivtection_Min_20.0Prob_quantile_comparison.svg" id="A6.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/368;" width="548" height="368"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>MIN-K%</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A6.F8.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09717v1/gpt_neox_20b_arxivtection_Modified_entropy_quantile_comparison.svg" id="A6.F8.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/368;" width="548" height="368"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>M-Entropy</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>FDR (solid lines) and power (bars) achieved by our method varying the hyperparameter <math id="A6.F8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> that is used to estimate data usage proportion <math id="A6.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>From Single-Point Inference to Multiple Testing: The MIA Dilemma</h2>

<section id="A7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.1 </span>The Ideal: A Rigorous Single-Point Test</h3>

<div id="A7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a data point <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and a trained target model <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, membership inference attacks (MIAs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shokri et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeom et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salem et al., 2019</a>)</cite> aim to identify whether <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is one of the members in the training set <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This type of privacy attack is often modeled as a statistical hypothesis testing problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bertran et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zarifzadeh et al., 2024</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="A7.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A7.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}:X\notin\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}\quad\text{v.s.}\quad H_{1}:X\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>v.s.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}:X\notin\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}\quad\text{v.s.}\quad H_{1}:X\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A7.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, the null hypothesis (<math id="A7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>) posits that <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-member, meaning it was drawn from the same underlying data distribution as <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> but was not included in it. Conversely, the alternative hypothesis (<math id="A7.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) posits that <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a member of the training set.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To reject the null hypothesis, we compute membership inference attack (MIA) scores, such as the model’s loss or confidence on data point <math id="A7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, let <math id="A7.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(X,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(X,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> represent the loss of <math id="A7.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> produced by model <math id="A7.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, then we can reject <math id="A7.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A7.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(X,\theta)\leqslant\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(X,\theta)\leqslant\tau</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">†</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Lower loss suggests that <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> was likely to be a part of the training set.</span></span></span>. To control the type I error, which is identical to the false positive rate (FPR) at the sample level, we choose <math id="A7.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="A7.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A7.E20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pr_{\theta\sim\Theta_{0}}[T(X,\theta)\leqslant\tau]\leqslant\alpha" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr_{\theta\sim\Theta_{0}}[T(X,\theta)\leqslant\tau]\leqslant\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A7.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A7.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the distribution over model parameters when trained on datasets that do not contain <math id="A7.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> (under <math id="A7.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="A7.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is the threshold used to define the rejection region.
In practice, sampling <math id="A7.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> typically requires training multiple reference models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2022</a>)</cite> or constructing Bayesian neural networks from a single reference model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, estimating this reject region is challenging for large-scale models, such as ChatGPT or DALL-E, due to limited access to the training data distribution and the training algorithm, compounded by the prohibitively high computational cost of training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.2 </span>The Reality: A Heuristic with a Conceptual Flaw</h3>

<div id="A7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several studies on MIAs applied to large language models and vision-language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> report true positive rates (TPR) at low FPRs using only MIA scores from the target model. This is typically achieved via a heuristic method: a single score threshold <math id="A7.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is determined on a calibration set of non-members to control the average FPR<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Formally, this metric can be written as the expected Type I error:</p>
<table id="A7.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A7.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FPR}=\mathbb{E}_{X}\left[\Pr[s(X,\theta_{1})\leqslant\tau\mid H_{0}]\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>FPR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FPR}=\mathbb{E}_{X}\left[\Pr[s(X,\theta_{1})\leqslant\tau\mid H_{0}]\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Herein lies a subtle but critical conceptual flaw. The average FPR conflates the overall error rate with the per-hypothesis Type I error rate, <math id="A7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr[T(X,\theta_{1})\leqslant\tau\mid H_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr[T(X,\theta_{1})\leqslant\tau\mid H_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>. As an average metric, it does not provide a probabilistic guarantee for any single inference. A low average FPR can mask a much higher error rate for specific subgroups of data, offering no reliable evidence for any individual decision.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, in practical scenarios like copyright infringement litigation, where the goal is to identify a reliable set of members from many candidates, the average FPR remains inappropriate. This scenario is a classic multiple testing problem. In this setting, the objective is not to manage an average error rate over all non-members, but to control the fraction of incorrect claims among the discoveries made. This is precisely the quantity measured by the False Discovery Rate (FDR), the statistically sound tool for this task.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The distinction between these metrics is critical in practice. Consider an audit of one million candidates containing only 1,000 true members. A method with seemingly excellent performance, such as a 0.1% average FPR and 80% TPR, would nevertheless yield approximately 999 false positives alongside 800 true positives. The resulting set’s FDR would be an untenable 55.5%, meaning over half the presented evidence is incorrect. This starkly demonstrates that average FPR is a fundamentally inadequate and misleading metric for ensuring the credibility of membership inference claims in a legal or auditing context.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
